        "original_id": "55775fa3-4f51-5d53-a35f-4ee4dd02f441",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Steven Weinberg, Abdus Salam, Sheldon Glashow\n- Roles: theoretical physicist, Nobel laureate, professor, board member\n- Organizations: Boston University, Harvard University, Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\n- Concepts: electroweak unification, asymptotic safety, quantum gravity, ultraviolet completion, nontrivial fixed point, nonrenormalizable field theories\n\nFacts:\n- Steven Weinberg was born on May 3, 1933.\n- Steven Weinberg is an American theoretical physicist and a Nobel laureate in Physics.\n- Steven Weinberg, Abdus Salam, and Sheldon Glashow received recognition for contributions to the unification of the weak force and electromagnetic interaction between elementary particles.\n- Sheldon Glashow is the Metcalf Professor of Mathematics and Physics at Boston University.\n- Sheldon Glashow is the Higgins Professor of Physics, Emeritus, at Harvard University.\n- Sheldon Glashow is a member of the Board of Sponsors for the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists.\n- Steven Weinberg originally proposed asymptotic safety to find a theory of quantum gravity.\n- The concept of a nontrivial fixed point as a possible ultraviolet completion can also be applied to other field theories, especially perturbatively nonrenormalizable ones.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
